#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cholesky

#% text
# `cholesky(m)` gives the upper triangular `u` with `m == dot(conjugate_transpose(u), u)`, for a positive definite matrix `m`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `m` must be Hermitian (symmetric for real entries) and positive definite; otherwise the call fails.

#% item
# Exact matrices give exact factors, with square roots where needed.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
cholesky([[4, 2], [2, 3]])
#% output
#= [[2, 1], [0, 2^(1/2)]]

#% cell
cholesky([[2.0, 1.0], [1.0, 2.0]])
#% output
#= [[1.4142135623730951, 0.7071067811865475], [0.0, 1.224744871391589]]

#% cell
cholesky([[1, 1, 1], [1, 2, 2], [1, 2, 3]])
#% output
#= [[1, 1, 1], [0, 1, 1], [0, 0, 1]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The factor gives the matrix back:

#% cell
m = [[4, 2], [2, 3]]; u = cholesky(m); dot(conjugate_transpose(u), u) == m
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A matrix that is not positive definite:

#% cell
cholesky([[1, 2], [2, 1]])
#% output
#! failure(not_positive_definite, cholesky)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `lu`, `qr`, `det`
